Rennari Java
============

Java implementation of the Rennari protocol: a small, secure, MessagePack-
or JSON-framed WebSocket event layer with custom events, rooms,
namespaces, acknowledgements, and connection state recovery.

Wire-compatible with Mojo::Rennari::Server (Perl), rennari-server-js
(Node), rennari-go, rennari-py, rennari-rs, and rennari-client-js.

Client and server live in one Maven artifact, as is idiomatic for Java.

STATUS

Working. Client and server tested against each other.

INSTALL

Add to your pom.xml:

    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.rennari</groupId>
        <artifactId>rennari</artifactId>
        <version>0.1.0</version>
    </dependency>

Or with Gradle:

    implementation 'com.rennari:rennari:0.1.0'

Requires Java 17 or newer.

QUICK START

Server:

    import com.rennari.Server;
    import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
    import java.util.Map;

    public class App {
        public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
            Server.Options opts = new Server.Options();
            opts.mock = true;

            Server server = new Server(opts);

            server.on("chat", (ctx, p) -> {
                Map<?, ?> m = (Map<?, ?>) p;
                String room = (String) m.get("room");
                ctx.join(room);
                ctx.broadcastToRoom(room, "chat", p);
                return null;
            });

            var ws = server.build(new InetSocketAddress("127.0.0.1", 3000));
            ws.start();
            Thread.currentThread().join();
        }
    }

Client:

    import com.rennari.Client;
    import java.util.Map;

    public class ClientApp {
        public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
            Client c = new Client(
                "ws://127.0.0.1:3000/ws",
                new Client.Options()
            );

            c.on("chat", p -> System.out.println("chat: " + p));
            c.connect();
            c.of("/").emit("chat", Map.of("room", "lobby", "text", "hi"));

            Thread.sleep(500);
            c.disconnect();
        }
    }

CORE CONCEPTS

Frames

Every message is a map:

    { e, d, id?, o?, n? }

- e - event name, [A-Za-z0-9_.:-]{1,64}
- d - payload
- id - optional acknowledgement id
- o - optional offset, present when recovery is enabled
- n - optional namespace, omitted for /

Serialized as MessagePack by default, or JSON if configured. Binary
WebSocket frames, always.

Classes

- com.rennari.Frame      - the frame model
- com.rennari.Protocol   - encode, decode, validation
- com.rennari.Server     - server, options, context, handler
- com.rennari.Client     - client, options, namespace

Server: authentication

AuthCb receives the ClientHandshake from Java-WebSocket. Return a
non-null identity map to accept, null to reject with close code 1008.

    opts.authCb = handshake -> {
        String path = handshake.getResourceDescriptor();
        String token = parseToken(path);
        return tokens.get(token);
    };

Server: events

    server.on("smile", (ctx, p) -> Map.of("ok", 1));

If the handler returns a non-null value, it is sent back as
{e: 'reply', d: value}.

Handlers are synchronous. To do async work, spawn a thread and use the
Context.

Server: sends

    ctx.sendToSelf("event", payload);
    ctx.sendTo(id, "event", payload);
    ctx.broadcast("event", payload);
    ctx.broadcastToRoom("lobby", "chat", payload);

Server: rooms

    ctx.join("lobby");
    ctx.leave("lobby");
    ctx.membersOf("lobby");   // List<Long>
    ctx.rooms();              // List<String>

Rooms are per-namespace. Auto-create on first join, disappear when the
last member leaves. No policy in the framework.

Server: namespaces

    server.namespace("/chat");
    server.addHandler("/chat", "hello", (ctx, p) -> {
        ctx.sendToSelf("hello_back", p);
        return null;
    });

A client joins with {e: 'rennari_nsp', d: {name: '/chat'}}. The server
replies rennari_nsp_ok. Frames for a namespace the client has not joined
close the connection with code 4005.

Server: acknowledgements

Client sends id with an event. Server replies {e: 'rennari_ack',
d: {id}} after the handler returns. No ack if the handler throws; the
connection closes with 4004.

Server: lifecycle hooks

    server.onConnect((ctx, identity) -> { ... return null; });
    server.onDisconnect((ctx, identity) -> { ... return null; });

Server: recovery

    Server.Options opts = new Server.Options();
    opts.recovery = true;
    opts.recoveryWindowSecs = 120;

Off by default. When on, the server issues a session id at handshake,
tracks an offset on every outbound frame, and buffers frames for a
connection that has dropped. On reconnect, the client sends
{e: 'rennari_resume', d: {session_id, offset}}. The server replays missed
frames, re-attaches namespaces and rooms, and replies rennari_resumed.

Server: mock mode

    Server.Options opts = new Server.Options();
    opts.mock = true;

Skips auth, synthesizes an identity, ignores unknown events. Development
only.

CLOSE CODES

- 4002 - bad frame
- 4003 - unknown event
- 4004 - handler error
- 4005 - unknown namespace
- 4006 - recovery disabled but client sent rennari_resume

CLIENT

    Client.Options opts = new Client.Options();
    opts.serializer = Protocol.Serializer.MSGPACK;
    opts.maxBytes = 64 * 1024;
    opts.ackTimeoutMs = 5000;
    opts.retries = 3;
    opts.extraHeaders = Map.of("Origin", "https://example.com");

    Client c = new Client(url, opts);
    c.on("event", handler);
    c.of("/chat").on("message", handler);
    c.connect();
    c.of("/").emit("event", payload);
    c.of("/chat").emit("message", payload);
    c.of("/").emitWithAck("ping", payload);
    c.disconnect();
    c.sessionId();      // String, null if not set
    c.lastOffset();     // long, -1 if not set

The client declares all non-default namespaces it has registered
immediately after connecting.

SECURITY

- authCb runs before the connection is accepted. A null return closes
  with 1008.
- Frames are validated on decode: event name grammar, id format, offset
  format, namespace grammar, size limit.
- Decoder throws, never returns garbage.
- Serializer is fixed per server or client.
- Mock mode is development-only; there is no production refusal guard
  like the JS and Perl servers have. Do not set mock in production.

The framework does not implement rate limiting. Add per-connection
budgets at the application level or use a reverse proxy.

EXAMPLES

examples/App.java runs a standalone server. examples/ClientApp.java runs
a client against it.

    mvn exec:java -Dexec.mainClass=com.rennari.examples.App
    mvn exec:java -Dexec.mainClass=com.rennari.examples.ClientApp

